# Stepper Motor Controller Simulation

## Problem Description
Implement a class to simulate the control of a stepper motor with acceleration/deceleration profiles. The controller should manage both targeted movements (moving to specific positions) and continuous movements (running at constant speed).

## Class Requirements
Implement a `StepperController` class with the following exact specifications:

```cpp
class StepperController {
private:
    // Motor parameters
    int dirPin;
    int enablePin;
    int stepPin;
    int microSteps;
    int stepsPerRev;
    double gearRatio;

    // Movement parameters
    double currentPosition;  // in revolutions
    double targetPosition;
    double currentSpeed;     // in rev/s
    double targetSpeed;
    double acceleration;     // in rev/s²
    bool isEnabled;
    bool isMoving;
    bool isContinuous;

public:
    StepperController(int dirPin, int enablePin, int stepPin, 
                     int microSteps = 1, int stepsPerRev = 200, double gearRatio = 1.0);

    void enable();
    void disable();

    void setCurrentPosition(double newPos);
    double getCurrentPosition() const;

    void setSpeed(double speedRevPerSec);
    double getCurrentSpeed() const;

    void setAcceleration(double accelRevPerSecSq);

    void move(double revolutions);
    void moveTo(double position);

    void runForward();
    void runBackward();

    void stopMove();
    void forceStop();

    void update(double deltaTime);

    map<string, double> getStatus() const;
};
```

## Member Function Specifications

### Constructor
```cpp
StepperController(int dirPin, int enablePin, int stepPin, 
                 int microSteps = 1, int stepsPerRev = 200, double gearRatio = 1.0)
```
- Initializes a new stepper controller with the given pin assignments and motor parameters
- Default parameters: microSteps=1, stepsPerRev=200, gearRatio=1.0
- Initial state: disabled, not moving, position/speed at 0

### Basic Control
```cpp
void enable()
```
- Enables the motor (allows movement)

```cpp
void disable()
```
- Disables the motor (stops all movement)

### Position Management
```cpp
void setCurrentPosition(double newPos)
```
- Sets the current position (in revolutions) if not moving

```cpp
double getCurrentPosition() const
```
- Returns current position in revolutions

### Speed Control
```cpp
void setSpeed(double speedRevPerSec)
```
- Sets target speed in revolutions per second

```cpp
double getCurrentSpeed() const
```
- Returns current speed in revolutions per second

### Acceleration Control
```cpp
void setAcceleration(double accelRevPerSecSq)
```
- Sets acceleration/deceleration rate in revolutions per second squared

### Movement Commands
```cpp
void move(double revolutions)
```
- Moves motor by specified number of revolutions (relative movement)
- Uses acceleration/deceleration profile
- Only works when enabled

```cpp
void moveTo(double position)
```
- Moves motor to absolute position (in revolutions)

```cpp
void runForward()
```
- Starts continuous forward movement at target speed

```cpp
void runBackward()
```
- Starts continuous backward movement at target speed

### Stopping
```cpp
void stopMove()
```
- Stops movement gracefully using deceleration profile

```cpp
void forceStop()
```
- Stops movement immediately (no deceleration)

### Update
```cpp
void update(double deltaTime)
```
- Updates motor position and speed based on elapsed time
- Must be called regularly to simulate movement
- Handles acceleration/deceleration automatically

### Status Reporting
```cpp
map<string, double> getStatus() const
```
- Returns a map containing all current movement parameters:
  - "position": current position (revolutions)
  - "speed": current speed (rev/s)
  - "target_position": target position (revolutions)
  - "target_speed": target speed (rev/s)
  - "acceleration": current acceleration (rev/s²)
  - "is_enabled": enabled status (1 or 0)
  - "is_moving": moving status (1 or 0)
  - "is_continuous": continuous movement status (1 or 0)

## Example Usage

```cpp
StepperController motor(2, 3, 4, 8, 200, 2.0);

motor.enable();
motor.setSpeed(1.5);         // Set target speed to 1.5 rev/s
motor.setAcceleration(0.5);  // Set acceleration to 0.5 rev/s²

// Move 5 revolutions forward
motor.move(5.0);

// Simulate time passing in 0.1 second increments
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    motor.update(0.1);
    auto status = motor.getStatus();
    cout << "Position: " << status["position"] 
         << " Speed: " << status["speed"] << endl;
}

// Switch to continuous movement
