# TrieMap Implementation Problem

## Problem Description
Implement a `TrieMap` class template that efficiently stores key-value pairs where keys are strings. The class should support standard map operations (insertion, lookup, removal) as well as prefix-based searches and longest prefix matching. The implementation must use a trie (prefix tree) data structure for efficient string operations.

## Class Requirements
Your implementation must exactly match the following class definition:

```cpp
template <typename T>
class TrieMap {
private:
    struct TrieNode {
        std::map<char, TrieNode*> children;
        bool isEnd = false;
        T value;
    };

    TrieNode* root;
    size_t count = 0;

    void clearHelper(TrieNode* node);
    TrieNode* cloneHelper(const TrieNode* node);
    void collectKeys(TrieNode* node, const std::string& prefix, std::vector<std::string>& result) const;

public:
    TrieMap();
    TrieMap(const TrieMap& other);
    ~TrieMap();
    TrieMap& operator=(const TrieMap& other);

    bool contains(const std::string& key) const;
    T& operator[](const std::string& key);
    void remove(const std::string& key);
    std::vector<std::string> keysWithPrefix(const std::string& prefix) const;
    std::string longestPrefixOf(const std::string& query) const;
    size_t size() const;
    bool empty() const;
    void clear();
};
```

## Function Specifications

### Constructors and Destructors
- `TrieMap()`: Default constructor creates an empty trie.
- `TrieMap(const TrieMap& other)`: Copy constructor.
- `~TrieMap()`: Destructor that cleans up all allocated memory.
- `operator=(const TrieMap& other)`: Assignment operator.

### Core Operations
- `bool contains(const std::string& key) const`: Returns true if the key exists in the trie.
- `T& operator[](const std::string& key)`: Returns a reference to the value associated with the key, inserting the key if it doesn't exist.
- `void remove(const std::string& key)`: Removes the key from the trie if it exists.

### Prefix Operations
- `std::vector<std::string> keysWithPrefix(const std::string& prefix) const`: Returns all keys in the trie that start with the given prefix.
- `std::string longestPrefixOf(const std::string& query) const`: Returns the longest key that is a prefix of the query string.

### Utility Functions
- `size_t size() const`: Returns the number of keys in the trie.
- `bool empty() const`: Returns true if the trie contains no keys.
- `void clear()`: Removes all keys from the trie.

## Constraints
1. Keys are case-sensitive strings (empty string is allowed)
2. All operations must maintain the trie structure correctly
3. Memory must be managed properly (no leaks)
4. The implementation must handle template type T correctly

## Example Usage
```cpp
TrieMap<int> wordCounts;
wordCounts["apple"] = 5;
wordCounts["appetite"] = 3;
wordCounts["banana"] = 2;

// Check existence
if (wordCounts.contains("apple")) {
    std::cout << "Found apple\n";
}

// Get all words starting with "app"
auto appWords = wordCounts.keysWithPrefix("app");
for (const auto& word : appWords) {
    std::cout << word << "\n";
}

// Find longest prefix
std::string prefix = wordCounts.longestPrefixOf("application");
std::cout << "Longest prefix: " << prefix << "\n";

// Remove a word
wordCounts.remove("banana");
```

## Evaluation Criteria
Your solution will be evaluated based on:
1. Correct implementation of all specified functions
2. Proper memory management
3. Handling of edge cases (empty strings, non-existent keys, etc.)
4. Efficiency of operations (should be O(L) for basic operations where L is key length)
5. Correct behavior when the trie is copied or assigned

## Notes
- You may add private helper functions as needed
- Do not modify the public interface
- The trie nodes must be cleaned up properly when the trie is destroyed
- The implementation should work for any valid template type T

