# User Management System

## Problem Description
Implement a User Management System that allows creating, authenticating, and managing user accounts. The system should track user login activity, manage account status (active/inactive), and provide password security analysis.

## Class Requirements
You must implement two classes with the exact specifications below:

### User Class
```cpp
class User {
public:
    User();
    User(string firstname, string lastname, string user_id, string password, int lcount = 0);

    // Getters
    string uid() const;
    string pwd() const;
    int hours() const;
    time_t getLastLogin() const;
    bool active() const;
    string fullName() const;

    // Authentication methods
    bool login(string pwd);
    void deactivate();
    void activate();
    void changePassword(string oldPwd, string newPwd);

    // Static methods
    static vector<User> makeUsersFrom(vector<string> userData);
    static map<string, string> generatePasswordReport(const vector<User>& users);

    // Operator overloads
    friend bool operator<(const User& lhs, const User& rhs);
    friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const User& user);

private:
    string fname, lname;
    string uid_, pwd_;
    int loginCount;
    time_t lastLogin;
    bool isActive;

    static vector<string> splitString(const string& s, char delimiter);
};
```

### UserManager Class
```cpp
class UserManager {
public:
    void addUser(const User& user);
    User* getUser(const string& uid);
    void removeUser(const string& uid);
    vector<User> getActiveUsers() const;
    void printAllUsers() const;

private:
    map<string, User> users;
};
```

## Functionality Requirements
1. **User Class**:
   - Store user details: first name, last name, user ID, password
   - Track login count and last login time
   - Manage account activation status
   - Provide authentication (login) functionality
   - Allow password changes with old password verification
   - Support bulk user creation from formatted strings
   - Generate password strength reports (weak/medium/strong)
   - Implement comparison and output stream operators

2. **UserManager Class**:
   - Add new users with unique IDs
   - Retrieve users by ID
   - Remove users
   - Get all active users
   - Print all users with their details

## Example Usage
```cpp
int main() {
    // Create users
    User user1("Alice", "Smith", "alice", "securePassword123");
    User user2("Bob", "Johnson", "bob", "bobspassword");

    // User authentication
    user1.login("wrong"); // returns false
    user1.login("securePassword123"); // returns true

    // Password change
    user2.changePassword("bobspassword", "newSecurePassword!");

    // User management
    UserManager manager;
    manager.addUser(user1);
    manager.addUser(user2);

    // Get active users
    vector<User> activeUsers = manager.getActiveUsers();

    // Bulk user creation
    vector<string> userData = {
        "Charlie,Brown,cbrown,charlie123,5",
        "Daisy,Johnson,djohnson,daisy456,10"
    };
    vector<User> newUsers = User::makeUsersFrom(userData);

    // Password report
    auto report = User::generatePasswordReport(activeUsers);

    return 0;
}
```

## Constraints
1. All class and function names must match exactly as specified
2. Member variables must remain private
3. Password strength classification:
   - weak: < 8 characters
   - medium: 8-11 characters
   - strong: ≥ 12 characters
4. User IDs must be unique in the UserManager
5. Password changes must verify the old password

## Notes
1. You may use standard C++ libraries
2. Pay attention to proper memory management
3. Handle edge cases (e.g., invalid user data formats)
4. The system should be thread-safe for single operations

