# Error Management System

## Problem Description
You are tasked with implementing an error management system for a compiler or interpreter. The system should track various types of errors that occur during program analysis, each with different severity levels and categories. The system should allow for adding contextual information to errors and provide various ways to organize and view the collected errors.

## Class Requirements
Implement the following classes exactly as specified:

### Error Class
```cpp
enum class ErrorSeverity {
    INFO,
    WARNING,
    ERROR,
    CRITICAL
};

enum class ErrorCategory {
    SYNTAX,
    SEMANTIC,
    RUNTIME,
    SYSTEM,
    NETWORK
};

class Error {
private:
    int error_num;
    string msg;
    int line;
    ErrorSeverity severity;
    ErrorCategory category;
    vector<string> context;

public:
    Error(int num, string _msg, int _line, 
          ErrorSeverity _severity = ErrorSeverity::ERROR, 
          ErrorCategory _category = ErrorCategory::SYNTAX);

    void add_context(const string& ctx);
    bool operator<(const Error& e) const;
    bool operator==(const Error& e) const;
    string format() const;
    int get_line() const;
    ErrorSeverity get_severity() const;
    ErrorCategory get_category() const;
};
```

### ErrorManager Class
```cpp
class ErrorManager {
private:
    vector<Error> errors;
    map<int, string> error_descriptions;

public:
    ErrorManager();
    void add_error(const Error& err);
    vector<Error> get_sorted_errors() const;
    vector<Error> get_errors_by_severity(ErrorSeverity severity) const;
    string get_error_description(int error_num) const;
    bool has_serious_errors() const;
};
```

## Requirements
1. The `Error` class should:
   - Store error details including error number, message, line number, severity, and category
   - Allow adding contextual information to errors
   - Support comparison operations based on line number and severity
   - Provide formatted output of error information
   - Include appropriate getter methods

2. The `ErrorManager` class should:
   - Maintain a collection of errors
   - Provide predefined descriptions for common error numbers
   - Support adding new errors
   - Provide sorted lists of errors (by line number and severity)
   - Allow filtering errors by severity level
   - Check for the presence of serious errors (ERROR or higher severity)

## Example Usage
```cpp
int main() {
    ErrorManager manager;

    // Add some errors
    Error syntaxError(101, "Missing semicolon", 10);
    Error typeError(200, "Type mismatch", 20, ErrorSeverity::ERROR, ErrorCategory::SEMANTIC);
    typeError.add_context("Expected: int");
    typeError.add_context("Found: string");

    manager.add_error(syntaxError);
    manager.add_error(typeError);

    // Get and display sorted errors
    auto allErrors = manager.get_sorted_errors();
    for (const auto& err : allErrors) {
        cout << err << endl;
    }

    // Check for serious errors
    if (manager.has_serious_errors()) {
        cout << "Serious errors detected!" << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

## Constraints
1. All class and member names must match exactly as specified
2. All function signatures must match exactly as specified
3. The implementation should handle all specified error severities and categories
4. The system should efficiently manage collections of errors
5. The formatted output should match the exact format shown in the examples

## Notes
- You may assume all input values are valid
