# Observer Pattern Implementation for News Distribution System

## Problem Description
You are tasked with implementing a news distribution system using the Observer design pattern. The system consists of a central news agency that publishes news updates, and multiple news channels that receive these updates automatically when they are published.

## Class Requirements
You need to implement the following classes with exact specifications:

### Observer (Abstract Base Class)
```cpp
class Observer {
public:
    virtual ~Observer() = default;
    virtual void update(const std::string& message) = 0;
    virtual std::string getName() const = 0;
};
```

### Observable (Base Class)
```cpp
class Observable {
protected:
    std::vector<Observer*> observers;
    std::string state;

public:
    virtual ~Observable() = default;

    void addObserver(Observer* observer);
    void removeObserver(Observer* observer);
    void notifyObservers();
    virtual void setState(const std::string& newState);
    std::string getState() const;
    size_t countObservers() const;
};
```

### NewsAgency (Derived from Observable)
```cpp
class NewsAgency : public Observable {
public:
    void publishNews(const std::string& news);
};
```

### NewsChannel (Derived from Observer)
```cpp
class NewsChannel : public Observer {
    std::string name;
    std::vector<std::string> receivedNews;

public:
    explicit NewsChannel(const std::string& name);

    void update(const std::string& message) override;
    std::string getName() const override;
    size_t newsCount() const;
    std::string getLatestNews() const;
    void clearNews();
};
```

## Implementation Requirements
1. The `Observable` class should maintain a list of observers and notify them when its state changes
2. The `NewsAgency` should prepend "BREAKING: " to all news items before publishing
3. Each `NewsChannel` should store all received news items and provide access to them
4. The system should handle adding/removing observers correctly
5. Null observers should be ignored

## Example Usage
```cpp
int main() {
    NewsAgency agency;
    NewsChannel channel1("CNN");
    NewsChannel channel2("BBC");

    // Subscribe channels to news agency
    agency.addObserver(&channel1);
    agency.addObserver(&channel2);

    // Publish news
    agency.publishNews("Earthquake reported in Pacific region");

    // Check results
    std::cout << "Agency state: " << agency.getState() << std::endl;
    std::cout << channel1.getName() << " latest news: " << channel1.getLatestNews() << std::endl;
    std::cout << channel2.getName() << " has received " << channel2.newsCount() << " news items" << std::endl;

    // Unsubscribe one channel
    agency.removeObserver(&channel1);
    agency.publishNews("Update: Earthquake magnitude revised");

    // Check updated results
    std::cout << channel1.getName() << " now has " << channel1.newsCount() << " news items" << std::endl;
    std::cout << channel2.getName() << " now has " << channel2.newsCount() << " news items" << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## Constraints
1. All class and function names must match exactly as specified
2. The implementation must follow the Observer pattern correctly
3. Memory management should be handled properly (no memory leaks)
4. The system should handle edge cases like null observers gracefully

## Evaluation Criteria
Your solution will be tested against multiple scenarios including:
1. Single observer registration and notification
2. Multiple observers receiving updates
3. Observer removal functionality
4. State management of both Observable and Observer classes
5. Edge cases with null observers and empty news items

Note: Do not modify the provided class interfaces or function signatures. Implement all the specified functionality exactly as described.

