# Word Frequency Tracker using AVL Tree

## Problem Description
Implement a word frequency tracker that maintains counts of words in a self-balancing AVL tree. The system should support adding words, removing words, and various query operations to analyze word frequencies.

## Class Requirements
You need to implement the following classes exactly as specified:

### Node Class
```cpp
class Node {
    string word;
    int count;
    Node *left, *right;
    int height;  // For AVL tree implementation

public:
    Node(const string& w) : word(w), count(1), left(nullptr), right(nullptr), height(1) {}

    string getWord() const { return word; }
    int getCount() const { return count; }
    void increment() { count++; }
    void decrement() { if (count > 0) count--; }

    friend class Tree;
    friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const Node& n);
};
```

### Tree Class
```cpp
class Tree {
    Node* root;

    // AVL tree helper functions
    int height(Node* n);
    int balanceFactor(Node* n);
    void updateHeight(Node* n);
    Node* rotateRight(Node* y);
    Node* rotateLeft(Node* x);
    Node* balance(Node* n);
    Node* insert(Node* node, const string& word);
    Node* minValueNode(Node* node);
    Node* remove(Node* root, const string& word);
    void inOrderTraversal(Node* n, vector<pair<string, int>>& result) const;
    void clear(Node* n);

public:
    Tree();
    ~Tree();

    void Add(const string& word);
    void Remove(const string& word);
    bool Find(const string& word) const;
    int getWordCount(const string& word) const;
    vector<pair<string, int>> getAllWords() const;
    int getTotalWordCount() const;
    int getDistinctWordCount() const;
    map<int, vector<string>> getWordsByFrequency() const;
    vector<string> getMostFrequentWords(int n) const;
    Node* getRoot() const;
};

ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const Tree& tree);
```

## Function Requirements
Implement all the member functions as shown in the class declarations above. The AVL tree must maintain balance after each insertion and deletion operation.

## Example Usage
```cpp
int main() {
    Tree wordTracker;

    // Add some words
    wordTracker.Add("apple");
    wordTracker.Add("banana");
    wordTracker.Add("apple");
    wordTracker.Add("cherry");

    // Check counts
    cout << "Total words: " << wordTracker.getTotalWordCount() << endl;
    cout << "Distinct words: " << wordTracker.getDistinctWordCount() << endl;
    cout << "Apple count: " << wordTracker.getWordCount("apple") << endl;

    // Get most frequent words
    vector<string> topWords = wordTracker.getMostFrequentWords(2);
    cout << "Top 2 words: ";
    for (const auto& word : topWords) {
        cout << word << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    // Remove a word
    wordTracker.Remove("banana");
    cout << "Banana count after removal: " << wordTracker.getWordCount("banana") << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## Input/Output Specifications
- Words are case-sensitive (e.g., "Word" and "word" are different)
- All word operations should maintain the AVL tree balance
- Query operations should return correct counts and statistics

## Constraints
- Maximum number of distinct words: 10,000
- Maximum length of each word: 100 characters
- Maximum total words: 1,000,000

## Notes
- The AVL tree must remain balanced after each insertion and deletion
- Word counts should never go below 0
- The system should efficiently handle all operations (O(log n) time complexity for tree operations)

## Evaluation Criteria
- Correct implementation of AVL tree balancing
- Accurate word counting and statistics
- Proper memory management
- Correct handling of edge cases (empty tree, duplicate words, etc.)

